cargo fmt drift recurs on every merge: three lanes have now landed unformatted files and each needed a central repair — nothing gates it


## RULING by Max, 2026-09-01 — resolved as process, not as a gate

> "tell agents to do it and if a worktree hasn't run fmt then you can do that
> before merging it in."

So the answer to the design question this ticket posed is **neither a gate nor a
lane-side reminder alone — it is both halves, with the coordinator as the
backstop:**

1. **Lanes run `cargo fmt --all` before reporting work as ready.** Cheap, no
   compilation, keeps a lane's diff reviewable.
2. **The coordinator runs it before merging a branch that skipped it**, and
   commits that repair **separately** from the lane's work.

**Deliberately NOT a gate.** Per B122's reasoning, which is unchanged: a
`--check` gate would fail every lane carrying a formatting nit mid-work, which is
most of them most of the time. And the failure mode here is a *reviewability*
problem, not a correctness one — it deserves a tidy-up, not a rejection.

Recorded in `AGENTS.md` (which `CLAUDE.md` symlinks to), so every future lane
inherits it without being told, and added to the coordinator's merge routine.

## Why the backstop is the load-bearing half

Three lanes landed unformatted work in one night (B146, B153, and one other), and
each needed a central repair. **A reminder alone would have caught none of them**
— every one of those lanes was diligent, and two had explicitly declined to
reformat another lane's files, which was the right call. The gap was structural:
nothing ran fmt on the merged result.

Second-order effect worth naming: unformatted files on master mean
`cargo fmt --all` **re-dirties them in every other lane's worktree**, so one
lane's omission shows up as spurious diff noise in everyone else's tree. That is
why this was recurring rather than incidental.